WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:13.376
Why does your face go the same way as a baby's when you taste something horrible? Nobody taught either of you. Darwin thought the same about many expressions.

00:00:13.960 --> 00:00:24.310
In eighteen seventy-two, Darwin argued that expressions in humans and animals are innate, which means born with, and inherited, which means passed on from parents. They are not learned.

00:00:24.880 --> 00:00:39.512
Why did they evolve? Through natural selection. Animals that signalled well, with warnings or threats, were more likely to survive and have young. So they passed the signals on.

00:00:40.080 --> 00:00:58.051
Some expressions once had a practical job. This is a serviceable habit. Baring teeth was getting ready to bite. Now it works as a warning. Narrowed eyes focus on a threat. Wide eyes in fear take in more of the surroundings.

00:00:58.640 --> 00:01:14.679
Many expressions, like anger, fear and disgust, look similar across cultures and in other primates. That supports evolution. A question gives a scene and asks you to explain signals, using Darwin.

00:01:15.800 --> 00:01:30.332
Step one: pick a specific signal from the stem. Step two: say what job it does, like a warning, a threat, or focusing. Step three: link it to Darwin: innate, inherited, and helped survival.

00:01:30.920 --> 00:01:43.904
For each way, one mark is the signal and its purpose. One mark is the Darwin justification. The traps are saying expressions are learned, and using the same Darwin sentence twice.

00:01:45.040 --> 00:02:04.953
Here is a real exam question. George and Edward argue about sport. George narrows his eyes without thinking, and later bares his teeth. We must explain two ways Darwin's theory can account for the non-verbal communication. Four marks.

00:02:05.520 --> 00:02:14.579
First choose two signals. George narrowed his eyes. George bared his teeth. Each one needs its job.

00:02:15.160 --> 00:02:28.638
Way one: narrowed eyes cut out the edges of vision, so George can concentrate. The stem says he did it instinctively. Darwin said such reactions are inherited, not learned.

00:02:29.200 --> 00:02:45.690
Way two: bared teeth. This warns Edward that George is ready to fight. Darwin called it a serviceable habit. Animals bare teeth before they bite. Those that warned rivals off survived.

00:02:46.240 --> 00:03:00.111
A tempting wrong route is to say George learned these faces by watching other people argue. Reject it. Darwin said expressions are inborn. The stem even says instinctively.

00:03:00.680 --> 00:03:04.992
The exam answer. Each way is the signal and its job, then Darwin.

00:03:05.560 --> 00:03:14.190
Each way earns one mark for the signal and its job, and one for the Darwin link. This explanation is by olevels dot com, not an official mark scheme.

00:03:15.320 --> 00:03:33.807
A common mistake is describing the behaviour without its purpose. Saying George bared his teeth earns little. Say it warned Edward off, and those who warned rivals off survived and passed that on.

00:03:34.920 --> 00:03:46.826
Now you try. Describe how non-verbal communication can be a product of evolution. Write your answer, then choose Continue to Answer.

00:03:47.960 --> 00:04:06.513
Non-verbal signals helped survival, like a baring of teeth to warn rivals. Animals that signalled well survived and had young. So the signals were inherited by later generations. That is natural selection, as Darwin said.

00:04:07.080 --> 00:04:16.430
A common wrong answer says expressions are learned from parents. That is the opposite of Darwin. He said they are innate and inherited.

00:04:17.000 --> 00:04:24.497
So, pick a signal. Say its job. Link it to Darwin: innate, inherited, and survival.
